### 🎯 Learning Objectives
- Implement default, no-argument, and parameterized constructors
- Chain constructors within the same class using this()
- Understand constructor overloading and automatic compiler defaults

### 📖 Concept Explanation
A **Constructor** is a special method called automatically when an object is instantiated. Key characteristics:
- It has the exact same name as the enclosing class.
- It has NO return type (not even void).
- If no constructor is written, the compiler generates a default no-argument constructor.
- If YOU write any constructor with parameters, the compiler no longer provides the default no-arg constructor unless explicitly defined.

### 💻 Syntax & Key Concepts
```java
public class Course {
    private String title;
    public Course() { this("Untitled"); } // Constructor chaining
    public Course(String title) { this.title = title; }
}
```

### 🚀 Hands-On Working Example
```java
public class ConstructorDemo {
    static class CourseModule {
        String title;
        int lessonCount;

        // Default constructor
        CourseModule() {
            this("Core Foundations", 10); // Constructor chaining
        }

        // Parameterized constructor
        CourseModule(String title, int lessonCount) {
            this.title = title;
            this.lessonCount = lessonCount;
        }

        void printInfo() {
            System.out.println("Module: '" + title + "' contains " + lessonCount + " lessons.");
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        CourseModule m1 = new CourseModule();
        CourseModule m2 = new CourseModule("Advanced OOP", 15);

        m1.printInfo();
        m2.printInfo();
    }
}
```

**Expected Output:**
```text
Module: 'Core Foundations' contains 10 lessons.
Module: 'Advanced OOP' contains 15 lessons.
```

### 🔍 Step-by-Step Walkthrough
1. m1 uses the default constructor which chains to the parameterized constructor via this(...).
2. m2 explicitly initializes title and lessonCount.
3. printInfo() outputs the initialized state of each instance.

### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid
- Accidentally adding a return type (e.g. `public void CourseModule()`), which turns the constructor into a regular method and causes confusion.
- Placing `this(...)` anywhere other than the very first line of a constructor body (causes compile error).

### 📝 Practice Questions & Challenges
1. **Why does `this(...)` have to be the first statement in a constructor?**
   *Hint:* Explain guaranteeing initialization before executing body statements.
2. **Does a class inherit constructors from its superclass?**
   *Hint:* Explain no, constructors are not inherited but invoked via super().
3. **What happens if you define a parameterized constructor but still need a no-arg constructor?**
   *Hint:* Explain needing to explicitly write the no-arg constructor.

