### 🎯 Learning Objectives
- Model books, members, and loans using OOP classes and collections
- Manage state transitions (available, borrowed, reserved)
- Enforce borrowing rules and handle custom domain exceptions

### 📖 Concept Explanation
In this project, we implement an Object-Oriented Library Management System:
- `Book`: Encapsulates ISBN, title, author, and checkout status.
- `Member`: Encapsulates member ID, name, and list of borrowed books.
- `Library`: Manages collections of books and members, handles checkouts, returns, and inventory queries.
- Custom exceptions: `BookNotAvailableException`, `MemberNotFoundException`.

### 💻 Syntax & Key Concepts
```java
public class Library {
    private Map<String, Book> catalog = new HashMap<>();
    public void checkoutBook(String isbn, String memberId) { ... }
}
```

### 🚀 Hands-On Working Example
```java
import java.util.*;

public class LibraryProjectDemo {
    static class Book {
        private final String isbn;
        private final String title;
        private boolean isBorrowed = false;

        Book(String isbn, String title) { this.isbn = isbn; this.title = title; }
        public String getIsbn() { return isbn; }
        public String getTitle() { return title; }
        public boolean isBorrowed() { return isBorrowed; }
        public void setBorrowed(boolean borrowed) { isBorrowed = borrowed; }
    }

    static class Library {
        private final Map<String, Book> catalog = new HashMap<>();

        public void addBook(Book b) { catalog.put(b.getIsbn(), b); }

        public void borrowBook(String isbn, String student) {
            Book b = catalog.get(isbn);
            if (b == null) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Book not found: " + isbn);
            if (b.isBorrowed()) throw new IllegalStateException("Book is currently on loan!");
            b.setBorrowed(true);
            System.out.println("Book '" + b.getTitle() + "' successfully checked out to " + student);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Library lib = new Library();
        lib.addBook(new Book("978-0134685991", "Effective Java"));

        lib.borrowBook("978-0134685991", "Alex Morgan");

        try {
            lib.borrowBook("978-0134685991", "Sarah Connor"); // Expect loan error
        } catch (IllegalStateException e) {
            System.out.println("Checkout rejected: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

**Expected Output:**
```text
Book 'Effective Java' successfully checked out to Alex Morgan
Checkout rejected: Book is currently on loan!
```

### 🔍 Step-by-Step Walkthrough
1. Encapsulates Book state and maintains catalog in HashMap.
2. borrowBook validates book existence and loan availability before updating state.
3. Rejects concurrent borrow attempts cleanly.

### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid
- Allowing a book to be checked out multiple times simultaneously.
- Failing to handle null when looking up non-existent ISBNs in the catalog map.

### 📝 Practice Questions & Challenges
1. **How would you add overdue fine calculations to this design?**
   *Hint:* Add checkout timestamp and compute elapsed days vs borrowing limit.
2. **How would you search books by author using Java 8 Streams?**
   *Hint:* Filter catalog.values().stream() matching author name.
3. **What collection would you use to maintain a waitlist for a popular book?**
   *Hint:* Queue<Member> / ArrayDeque<Member>.

